1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:05,141
This craft is landing near the Moon's
south pole, where no one has landed before.

2
00:00:05,181 --> 00:00:07,435
It cost less than a Hollywood space film.

3
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:13,982
In the same country, a snakebite kills
someone about every nine minutes.

4
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:19,625
Here, in a mountain lake five
kilometres up, lie skeletons.

5
00:00:19,665 --> 00:00:23,248
And the DNA of some of them
leads... to the Mediterranean.

6
00:00:23,288 --> 00:00:25,309
How they got there, nobody knows.

7
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:32,101
The Moon, snakes, and skeletons in the
Himalayas. All one country. Beautiful.

8
00:00:32,141 --> 00:00:33,607
Ancient. Hard. No filters.

9
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:38,766
Today we're flying to India.

10
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:50,459
First, the scale, something to hold on to.

11
00:00:50,499 --> 00:00:54,548
Nearly one and a half billion people
live here, about ten times Russia.

12
00:00:54,588 --> 00:00:57,370
Since two thousand
twenty-three, it's the world's

13
00:00:57,410 --> 00:00:59,558
most populous country, ahead of China.

14
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:05,019
Here's the first oddity
I'll ask you to remember.

15
00:01:05,059 --> 00:01:09,089
In a country everyone calls overpopulated,
women now have fewer children

16
00:01:09,129 --> 00:01:12,197
than needed just to keep the
population from shrinking.

17
00:01:12,237 --> 00:01:14,797
How both can be true,
I'll explain at the end.

18
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:21,495
One promise. I won't
show you only postcards.

19
00:01:21,535 --> 00:01:23,365
And I won't show you only dirt.

20
00:01:23,405 --> 00:01:26,683
The facts in this film are
checked against sources, and

21
00:01:26,723 --> 00:01:29,036
where scientists disagree, I'll say so.

22
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:34,984
We have nine stops. Animals
found nowhere else in the wild.

23
00:01:35,024 --> 00:01:37,817
A city with sewers four and
a half thousand years old.

24
00:01:37,857 --> 00:01:40,596
A mystery that was solved,
and one nobody has solved.

25
00:01:40,636 --> 00:01:43,376
And the other side, the one
adverts keep quiet about.

26
00:01:43,416 --> 00:01:46,156
Let's start at the very
beginning. Back when India...

27
00:01:46,196 --> 00:01:47,572
wasn't next to Asia at all.

28
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:04,704
The world's highest mountains came
from a crash. A very slow one.

29
00:02:04,744 --> 00:02:07,441
Picture the world over
fifty million years ago.

30
00:02:07,481 --> 00:02:11,982
India isn't part of Asia, but a huge
landmass drifting north across the ocean.

31
00:02:12,022 --> 00:02:14,253
Fast: up to sixteen centimetres a year.

32
00:02:14,293 --> 00:02:15,766
A sprint, for a continent.

33
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:23,678
About forty to fifty million years
ago, this island slammed into Asia.

34
00:02:23,718 --> 00:02:26,211
Not like a car into a
post, but like a rug pushed

35
00:02:26,251 --> 00:02:28,537
against a wall: the edge
crumples into folds.

36
00:02:28,577 --> 00:02:31,224
Those folds are the Himalayas.
The crash isn't over.

37
00:02:31,264 --> 00:02:34,171
India still pushes, now slower:
a few centimetres a year.

38
00:02:34,211 --> 00:02:35,463
The Himalayas still grow.

39
00:02:35,503 --> 00:02:36,858
By millimetres, every year.

40
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:47,045
That collision shaped almost
everything in this film.

41
00:02:47,085 --> 00:02:51,711
The mountains rose as a wall in the north,
catching moist ocean air: hence the rain.

42
00:02:51,751 --> 00:02:54,434
Their glaciers feed great
rivers, the Ganges too.

43
00:02:54,474 --> 00:02:57,545
Over centuries the rivers
laid down silt, a vast fertile

44
00:02:57,585 --> 00:02:59,989
plain where hundreds
of millions live today.

45
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:06,045
Remember this wall. We'll need it.

46
00:03:06,085 --> 00:03:09,779
Because in a later chapter, it turns
out to be not a shield... but a trap.

47
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:18,057
Now, a little game. Guess which of
these is also India. Hint: all of them.

48
00:03:18,097 --> 00:03:20,274
In the far north, in
Ladakh, a cold desert.

49
00:03:20,314 --> 00:03:23,627
Bare mountains, blue lakes above
four kilometres, almost no rain:

50
00:03:23,667 --> 00:03:26,154
the clouds can't get here,
the ranges block them.

51
00:03:26,194 --> 00:03:28,681
One of the highest, driest
places in the country.

52
00:03:28,721 --> 00:03:30,693
No trees, but the stars are very close.

53
00:03:39,370 --> 00:03:40,977
To the west, the Thar Desert.

54
00:03:41,017 --> 00:03:43,987
Sand, camels, and the yellow
stone city of Jaisalmer,

55
00:03:44,027 --> 00:03:46,316
which glows at sunset as if cast in gold.

56
00:03:46,356 --> 00:03:50,121
Now fly south in your mind, almost
two thousand kilometres. Kerala.

57
00:03:50,161 --> 00:03:54,721
Palm trees, thousands of canals, and
houseboats drifting slowly over green water.

58
00:03:54,761 --> 00:03:56,709
Same country. No connecting flight.

59
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:03,993
And in between, mountains covered
in tea like a green carpet.

60
00:04:04,033 --> 00:04:07,981
Women pick the leaves by hand, taking
only the tip: two leaves and a bud.

61
00:04:08,021 --> 00:04:11,587
And a salt lake where thousands
of pink flamingos stand in winter.

62
00:04:11,627 --> 00:04:13,335
Desert, glaciers, jungle, ocean.

63
00:04:13,375 --> 00:04:16,941
All made by one crash fifty million
years ago. Well, and the rain.

64
00:04:26,250 --> 00:04:28,976
Now, the rain. The
monsoon is India's breath.

65
00:04:29,016 --> 00:04:32,787
Months of dry heat, then ocean
wind arrives, heavy with water.

66
00:04:32,827 --> 00:04:35,676
And the waiting country
is soaked within weeks.

67
00:04:35,716 --> 00:04:38,196
From space, it looks like a giant inhale.

68
00:04:42,050 --> 00:04:45,733
The monsoon has an arrival date:
around early June it lands in

69
00:04:45,773 --> 00:04:48,976
Kerala, down south, and the
news greets it like a VIP.

70
00:04:49,016 --> 00:04:51,317
Then it moves north, to that same wall.

71
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:58,216
Now, a record. The northeastern
town of Cherrapunji.

72
00:04:58,256 --> 00:05:01,335
From summer eighteen sixty
to summer eighteen sixty-one,

73
00:05:01,375 --> 00:05:04,732
twenty-six and a half metres of
rain fell there. In one year.

74
00:05:04,772 --> 00:05:09,131
If it never drained away, it would bury
a nine-storey building. Up to the roof.

75
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:16,992
It's no fluke. Next door is the
village of Mawsynram, called one of

76
00:05:17,032 --> 00:05:19,075
the wettest inhabited places on Earth.

77
00:05:19,115 --> 00:05:22,418
On average, almost twelve metres
of water a year. Every year.

78
00:05:22,458 --> 00:05:24,775
Here people don't ask
whether it will rain.

79
00:05:24,815 --> 00:05:26,583
They ask when it'll briefly stop.

80
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:33,498
But the monsoon isn't always gentle.

81
00:05:33,538 --> 00:05:36,759
Here's Mumbai, the twenty-ninth
of August, two thousand seventeen.

82
00:05:36,799 --> 00:05:40,266
One day of heavy rain, and the streets
of a huge city turn into rivers.

83
00:05:40,306 --> 00:05:42,835
People wade waist-deep,
cars sit roof-deep in water.

84
00:05:42,875 --> 00:05:45,404
That downpour took lives
and stopped the whole city.

85
00:05:45,444 --> 00:05:46,935
A few days later, life went on.

86
00:05:46,975 --> 00:05:49,307
People here know how to
rise again after floods.

87
00:05:58,450 --> 00:06:00,980
Here, people stopped
fighting the rain long ago.

88
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:03,442
The Khasi don't build
bridges, they grow them.

89
00:06:03,482 --> 00:06:07,832
Fig roots trained across a river become,
in ten to fifteen years, a living bridge.

90
00:06:07,872 --> 00:06:09,170
Even a double-decker one.

91
00:06:09,210 --> 00:06:12,704
Since two thousand twenty-two,
they're on UNESCO's tentative list.

92
00:06:16,750 --> 00:06:18,660
But this land of rain has a flip side.

93
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:20,301
Before the monsoon, India bakes.

94
00:06:20,341 --> 00:06:22,764
In spring two thousand
twenty-four, thirty-seven

95
00:06:22,804 --> 00:06:24,867
cities topped forty-five degrees Celsius.

96
00:06:24,907 --> 00:06:28,715
Official heatstroke death tolls differed
many times over: from fifty-six in

97
00:06:28,755 --> 00:06:31,844
the first report to three hundred
and sixty in the final one.

98
00:06:31,884 --> 00:06:34,717
Independent researchers counted
more than seven hundred.

99
00:06:39,670 --> 00:06:43,618
The big paradox: the country with the
heaviest rains on Earth is short of water.

100
00:06:43,658 --> 00:06:46,560
A government think tank estimates
about six hundred million

101
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:48,654
Indians face high to extreme water stress.

102
00:06:48,694 --> 00:06:51,495
Almost forty percent of water
comes from underground, and

103
00:06:51,535 --> 00:06:53,789
satellites watch those
reserves shrink yearly.

104
00:06:53,829 --> 00:06:55,085
The land of record rain...

105
00:06:55,125 --> 00:06:56,431
queues for the water truck.

106
00:07:02,760 --> 00:07:06,193
But mountains, rain and rivers
didn't only create hardship.

107
00:07:06,233 --> 00:07:09,490
They created one of the last
truly wild worlds on Earth.

108
00:07:09,530 --> 00:07:13,551
And in it lives an animal that no
other country has left in the wild.

109
00:07:19,050 --> 00:07:21,630
Imagine meeting a wild
tiger anywhere on Earth.

110
00:07:21,670 --> 00:07:23,803
Three times in four, it'll be in India.

111
00:07:23,843 --> 00:07:26,701
The last census found over
three and a half thousand

112
00:07:26,741 --> 00:07:29,265
here, about three quarters
of all wild tigers.

113
00:07:29,305 --> 00:07:33,947
They're counted by camera traps: each tiger's
stripes are unique, like fingerprints.

114
00:07:38,370 --> 00:07:40,832
The tiger is India's national animal, seen

115
00:07:40,872 --> 00:07:43,632
everywhere: stamps, coins,
the sides of trucks.

116
00:07:43,672 --> 00:07:47,385
In the forest, though, its life
is mostly silence and patience.

117
00:07:47,425 --> 00:07:50,125
A tigress raises her
cubs for about two years,

118
00:07:50,165 --> 00:07:53,819
teaching them to hide, hunt and
stay unseen. Especially by us.

119
00:07:57,170 --> 00:07:58,784
But the total is misleading.

120
00:07:58,824 --> 00:08:03,567
The same report says more than a third of
tiger reserves need urgent restoration.

121
00:08:03,607 --> 00:08:08,233
And in one northeastern state, tigers
fell threefold: from twenty-nine to nine.

122
00:08:11,970 --> 00:08:14,151
Now, that animal. The Asiatic lion.

123
00:08:14,191 --> 00:08:17,006
It once roamed from the
Middle East to India.

124
00:08:17,046 --> 00:08:20,941
Today, in the wild, it survives
in exactly one place on Earth:

125
00:08:20,981 --> 00:08:23,923
Gujarat, the Gir forest
and the land around it.

126
00:08:27,610 --> 00:08:30,416
Look closely: it differs
from its African cousin.

127
00:08:30,456 --> 00:08:32,624
A shorter, thinner mane, ears showing.

128
00:08:32,664 --> 00:08:37,097
A fold of skin along the belly, like a
little apron. A modest king of beasts.

129
00:08:37,137 --> 00:08:38,084
But still a king.

130
00:08:41,940 --> 00:08:45,179
In two thousand one, there were
three hundred and twenty-seven.

131
00:08:45,219 --> 00:08:48,979
In two thousand twenty-five, eight
hundred and ninety-one. Nearly triple.

132
00:08:49,019 --> 00:08:52,571
Then, a surprise. More than half of
these lions now live outside Gir.

133
00:08:52,611 --> 00:08:54,966
They roam fields and
wasteland, past villages.

134
00:08:55,006 --> 00:08:56,631
Neighbours the whole area knows.

135
00:08:56,671 --> 00:08:58,089
And tries to steer clear of.

136
00:09:03,060 --> 00:09:05,098
Scientists question the census method.

137
00:09:05,138 --> 00:09:09,035
But the lions have clearly outgrown
the old forest, and seek a new home.

138
00:09:11,010 --> 00:09:13,842
Far to the east, in
Assam, is Kaziranga park.

139
00:09:13,882 --> 00:09:17,608
It's home to over two and a
half thousand Indian one-horned

140
00:09:17,648 --> 00:09:20,480
rhinos, out of about
four thousand worldwide.

141
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:23,735
Almost two thirds of the
species graze in one park.

142
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:31,374
India has about twenty-two thousand
wild elephants, first counted using DNA.

143
00:09:31,414 --> 00:09:35,727
Space is shrinking, and clashes with
people in fields and tea gardens

144
00:09:35,767 --> 00:09:39,385
are now among the worst problems
for forest-edge villages.

145
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:43,472
But here the elephant
isn't just a forest animal.

146
00:09:43,512 --> 00:09:46,855
It's faith too: Ganesha, god of
wisdom, has an elephant's head.

147
00:09:46,895 --> 00:09:51,259
Dressed-up elephants march in southern
festivals, and some temples keep their own.

148
00:09:51,299 --> 00:09:54,159
The wild elephant feared in
the field and the elephant

149
00:09:54,199 --> 00:09:57,167
god in the temple: one and
the same animal. Very Indian.

150
00:10:02,650 --> 00:10:04,948
Here's an animal almost nobody has seen.

151
00:10:04,988 --> 00:10:07,869
A dolphin lives in the
muddy Ganges. Almost blind.

152
00:10:07,909 --> 00:10:11,960
It doesn't look, it listens: clicking
and catching echoes, like a bat.

153
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:16,168
The first-ever census counted over
six thousand river dolphins in India.

154
00:10:16,208 --> 00:10:17,921
In a river we'll come back to.

155
00:10:22,850 --> 00:10:26,889
Others are easy to see. The peacock
is the national bird, and knows it.

156
00:10:26,929 --> 00:10:29,819
Langurs sit on rooftops
like pensioners on a bench.

157
00:10:29,859 --> 00:10:33,151
Temple macaques snatch your
bag before you can say 'oops'.

158
00:10:36,700 --> 00:10:39,725
Folk wisdom says: if a peacock
dances, rain is coming.

159
00:10:39,765 --> 00:10:42,392
Really, the male fans
his tail to win a female,

160
00:10:42,432 --> 00:10:45,740
and mating season falls in the
rains. So the sign is right.

161
00:10:45,780 --> 00:10:48,578
The peacock just isn't
thinking about the weather.

162
00:10:54,110 --> 00:10:57,284
At dusk in the Thar, a small
fox with huge ears emerges.

163
00:10:57,324 --> 00:11:00,039
They hear prey under the
sand, and keep it cool.

164
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:06,921
Now a number that usually
wipes the smile off.

165
00:11:06,961 --> 00:11:08,657
Remember the snakes from the start?

166
00:11:08,697 --> 00:11:12,277
India has four especially dangerous
species, known as the 'big four': the

167
00:11:12,317 --> 00:11:15,748
spectacled cobra, the krait, Russell's
viper and the saw-scaled viper.

168
00:11:15,788 --> 00:11:18,475
A major scientific study
estimates that snakebites kill

169
00:11:18,515 --> 00:11:20,756
about fifty-eight thousand
people here a year.

170
00:11:20,796 --> 00:11:22,987
That's one person roughly
every nine minutes.

171
00:11:29,660 --> 00:11:33,753
Why so many? Usually it's a village,
a field, the rainy season, and a

172
00:11:33,793 --> 00:11:36,089
long road to a hospital with antivenom.

173
00:11:36,129 --> 00:11:40,521
Half the deaths happen in the monsoon,
June to September. One more detail.

174
00:11:40,561 --> 00:11:43,695
Over twelve years, official
statistics recorded about

175
00:11:43,735 --> 00:11:46,330
fifteen and a half
thousand hospital deaths.

176
00:11:46,370 --> 00:11:51,302
Researchers estimated about one hundred
and fifty-four thousand in hospitals alone.

177
00:11:51,342 --> 00:11:55,135
Ten times more. And three deaths
in four happen outside hospital

178
00:11:55,175 --> 00:11:57,950
altogether: people simply
don't reach a doctor.

179
00:12:02,700 --> 00:12:07,066
Animals, rivers, rain... People have
lived amid all this for a very long time.

180
00:12:07,106 --> 00:12:10,341
So long that four and a half
thousand years ago, they were

181
00:12:10,381 --> 00:12:12,714
already laying sewers under their streets.

182
00:12:12,754 --> 00:12:15,707
And writing, in a script
nobody can read to this day.

183
00:12:21,950 --> 00:12:23,618
The Indus Valley Civilisation.

184
00:12:23,658 --> 00:12:27,775
It flourished from roughly the
twenty-sixth to the nineteenth century BC.

185
00:12:27,815 --> 00:12:31,989
Its most famous cities, Mohenjo-daro
and Harappa, are in today's Pakistan.

186
00:12:32,029 --> 00:12:36,147
But India has its own. Like Dholavira
in Gujarat, a UNESCO World Heritage

187
00:12:36,187 --> 00:12:38,140
Site since two thousand twenty-one.

188
00:12:40,970 --> 00:12:42,060
Here's the amazing part.

189
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:45,403
Clay pipes ran from the houses to
shared brick drains under the street.

190
00:12:45,443 --> 00:12:49,169
The drains had removable covers, so they
could be cleaned. Sewers with manholes.

191
00:12:49,209 --> 00:12:50,817
Four and a half thousand years ago.

192
00:12:50,857 --> 00:12:53,312
There was also a huge pool:
twelve metres long, seven

193
00:12:53,352 --> 00:12:55,195
wide, almost two and a half metres deep.

194
00:12:55,235 --> 00:12:58,208
What it was for, ritual bathing
or ceremonies, is still debated.

195
00:13:08,410 --> 00:13:12,104
Now a riddle scientists have battled
for a century. These people wrote.

196
00:13:12,144 --> 00:13:14,734
Their signs, on small stone
seals: little figures,

197
00:13:14,774 --> 00:13:16,785
fish, strokes, and animals beside them.

198
00:13:16,825 --> 00:13:18,836
Yet nobody can read them. Not one word.

199
00:13:18,876 --> 00:13:21,781
We know how they built, what
they ate, what they traded.

200
00:13:21,821 --> 00:13:23,674
But not what they called themselves.

201
00:13:29,870 --> 00:13:31,740
Other ancient words reached us alive.

202
00:13:31,780 --> 00:13:33,909
The Vedas, Hinduism's oldest sacred texts.

203
00:13:33,949 --> 00:13:36,904
The oldest, the Rigveda, is
over three thousand years old.

204
00:13:36,944 --> 00:13:40,002
For millennia they passed not
on paper but by voice: teacher

205
00:13:40,042 --> 00:13:42,429
to student, word for
word, even the intonation.

206
00:13:42,469 --> 00:13:45,217
Hear a priest chant a hymn
today, and you may hear one

207
00:13:45,257 --> 00:13:47,179
of Earth's oldest unbroken traditions.

208
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:56,501
Another Indian thing you use every
day. Probably right now. Zero.

209
00:13:56,541 --> 00:14:00,262
Not the idea of emptiness, but the sign
itself: a circle or dot meaning 'nothing'.

210
00:14:00,302 --> 00:14:04,115
A manuscript found in Bakhshali village
holds one of the oldest known written zeros.

211
00:14:04,155 --> 00:14:06,867
Some pages were dated to the
third or fourth century, though

212
00:14:06,907 --> 00:14:09,023
scholars argue the pages
seem to differ in age.

213
00:14:09,063 --> 00:14:12,508
Still, almost nobody doubts zero as a
digit reached the world through India.

214
00:14:23,470 --> 00:14:26,080
And now the most
impossible building I know.

215
00:14:26,120 --> 00:14:29,513
Usually a temple is built:
stone on stone, bottom to top.

216
00:14:29,553 --> 00:14:34,029
The Kailasa temple at Ellora was done
the other way round. It wasn't built.

217
00:14:34,069 --> 00:14:36,679
It was carved. Top down.
From a single rock.

218
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:43,866
Imagine: you have a mountain.

219
00:14:43,906 --> 00:14:46,261
You must see a temple inside
it, with columns, stairs,

220
00:14:46,301 --> 00:14:49,410
life-size elephants, and remove
everything else. Every blow is forever.

221
00:14:49,450 --> 00:14:51,672
Make a mistake, and you
can't stick the stone back.

222
00:14:51,712 --> 00:14:55,175
Estimates say two hundred to four hundred
thousand tonnes of rock were removed.

223
00:14:55,215 --> 00:14:58,635
Two hundred thousand tonnes is about
twenty Eiffel Towers. Not a single brick.

224
00:14:58,675 --> 00:14:59,743
Only what was taken away.

225
00:15:11,130 --> 00:15:12,644
This was the eighth century.

226
00:15:12,684 --> 00:15:14,863
No computer drawings, no room for error.

227
00:15:14,903 --> 00:15:19,413
If you ever find yourself in India with
just one day, honestly, I'd spend it here.

228
00:15:23,170 --> 00:15:26,463
Down south in Thanjavur stands another
giant: the Brihadeeswarar temple.

229
00:15:26,503 --> 00:15:29,101
Built about a thousand years
ago under the Chola dynasty.

230
00:15:29,141 --> 00:15:32,157
Not carved rock, but the opposite:
a huge granite tower of blocks.

231
00:15:32,197 --> 00:15:35,489
It's crowned by a massive stone dome,
said to weigh about eighty tonnes.

232
00:15:35,529 --> 00:15:37,850
How they lifted it without
cranes is still debated.

233
00:15:37,890 --> 00:15:40,813
It's no museum: people pray here
daily, as a thousand years ago.

234
00:15:50,120 --> 00:15:55,081
In the east, on the Bay of Bengal, is
Konark's Sun Temple. Thirteenth century.

235
00:15:55,121 --> 00:16:00,402
The whole temple is the Sun god's stone
chariot, on twenty-four huge carved wheels.

236
00:16:00,442 --> 00:16:03,800
Some are sundials: the
spokes' shadow tells the time.

237
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:08,710
Locals say a giant magnet
once sat on top of the

238
00:16:08,750 --> 00:16:11,976
temple, so strong it pulled
ships off course. A lovely story.

239
00:16:12,016 --> 00:16:15,992
But there's no trace of that magnet,
in records or in stone. It's a legend.

240
00:16:16,032 --> 00:16:18,080
India does have real mysteries, though.

241
00:16:18,120 --> 00:16:21,025
One was solved long ago, but
the answer sounds made up.

242
00:16:21,065 --> 00:16:23,220
The other, nobody has solved to this day.

243
00:16:33,340 --> 00:16:37,419
First, the solved one. Delhi, the Qutub
Minar complex. See that dark pillar?

244
00:16:37,459 --> 00:16:40,833
It's iron, cast around the fifth
century, about sixteen hundred

245
00:16:40,873 --> 00:16:43,435
years ago, and has stood
in the open ever since.

246
00:16:43,475 --> 00:16:46,470
Sixteen centuries of monsoon
rains. And it barely rusts.

247
00:16:46,510 --> 00:16:49,559
Ordinary iron would long since
have crumbled to red dust.

248
00:16:54,730 --> 00:16:59,041
The answer came from a metallurgist at the
Indian Institute of Technology in Kanpur.

249
00:16:59,081 --> 00:17:02,459
The iron is rich in phosphorus,
many times more than modern steel.

250
00:17:02,499 --> 00:17:06,499
Phosphorus helps form a thin, dense film
sealing the metal from water and air.

251
00:17:06,539 --> 00:17:08,363
The smiths likely knew no chemistry.

252
00:17:08,403 --> 00:17:10,021
They simply made iron their way.

253
00:17:10,061 --> 00:17:12,196
And made it to last sixteen hundred years.

254
00:17:19,180 --> 00:17:22,155
Here's the trace of a catastrophe,
in plain sight. Maharashtra state.

255
00:17:22,195 --> 00:17:24,164
Amid a flat plain, a
perfectly round lake in a

256
00:17:24,204 --> 00:17:26,786
stone bowl almost two kilometres
wide. This is Lonar crater.

257
00:17:26,826 --> 00:17:29,931
About five hundred and seventy thousand
years ago, a meteorite hit here.

258
00:17:29,971 --> 00:17:32,858
And the crater is rare: punched
into hardened ancient lava, basalt.

259
00:17:32,898 --> 00:17:35,218
Scientists study it to
understand Moon craters better.

260
00:17:35,258 --> 00:17:38,145
And its water is salty and alkaline,
nothing like the local rivers.

261
00:17:49,530 --> 00:17:52,637
Here's a temple whose mystery lies
not in stone, but in its residents.

262
00:17:52,677 --> 00:17:55,740
The Karni Mata temple in Rajasthan
houses about twenty thousand rats.

263
00:17:55,780 --> 00:17:57,898
Nobody poisons them:
they're fed milk and grain.

264
00:17:57,938 --> 00:18:00,236
Locals believe they're a
saint's reborn descendants.

265
00:18:00,276 --> 00:18:03,878
Spot a white one among thousands of grey
rats, and it's said to bring great luck.

266
00:18:03,918 --> 00:18:06,576
Pilgrims, as in any Indian
temple, walk barefoot among rats.

267
00:18:06,616 --> 00:18:08,375
To them, it's a blessing, not an ordeal.

268
00:18:19,260 --> 00:18:21,833
Sometimes a legend is just
misunderstood science.

269
00:18:21,873 --> 00:18:25,407
In Jatinga village, in Assam, they
say birds come to die: on foggy,

270
00:18:25,447 --> 00:18:29,887
moonless autumn nights, they fall from the
sky onto lights. Legend calls it suicide.

271
00:18:29,927 --> 00:18:32,713
Ornithologists disagree: in
fog and wind, young local

272
00:18:32,753 --> 00:18:37,087
birds lose their bearings and fly to the
light. No mysticism. Just fog and a lamp.

273
00:18:42,950 --> 00:18:45,439
Here's a place where legend
holds tighter than fact.

274
00:18:45,479 --> 00:18:48,212
The Thar Desert, near Jaisalmer.
The village of Kuldhara.

275
00:18:48,252 --> 00:18:49,768
Stone houses, streets, a temple.

276
00:18:49,808 --> 00:18:51,033
And not a single resident.

277
00:18:51,073 --> 00:18:54,048
Legend says one night the people
of this village and dozens of

278
00:18:54,088 --> 00:18:57,210
neighbouring ones all left at
once, fleeing a cruel minister, and

279
00:18:57,250 --> 00:18:59,933
cursed the place so no one
would ever settle here again.

280
00:19:08,460 --> 00:19:10,135
Historians tell a duller story.

281
00:19:10,175 --> 00:19:14,229
Paliwal Brahmins founded the village
around the thirteenth century, and in

282
00:19:14,269 --> 00:19:17,438
the nineteenth it emptied along
with dozens of neighbours.

283
00:19:17,478 --> 00:19:20,923
Probably heavy taxes, drought,
and fading desert caravan trade.

284
00:19:20,963 --> 00:19:24,464
But the curse did work. Just in
reverse: now tourists come here.

285
00:19:28,590 --> 00:19:29,587
Now, one for UFO fans.

286
00:19:29,627 --> 00:19:33,075
High in Ladakh, on the disputed
India-China border, is the Kongka La pass.

287
00:19:33,115 --> 00:19:35,526
Civilians aren't allowed:
it's a closed border zone.

288
00:19:35,566 --> 00:19:37,034
Where nobody goes, rumours grow.

289
00:19:37,074 --> 00:19:40,993
They say it hides a UFO base, and herders
see lights in the sky. No evidence at all.

290
00:19:41,033 --> 00:19:43,067
Just a border, soldiers
and a very dark sky.

291
00:19:43,107 --> 00:19:44,811
Sometimes that's enough for a legend.

292
00:19:54,690 --> 00:19:56,968
On the way there, near
Leh, is Magnetic Hill.

293
00:19:57,008 --> 00:20:00,934
Drivers stop, shift into neutral, and
the car seems to roll uphill by itself.

294
00:20:00,974 --> 00:20:02,530
Tourists film it, locals smile.

295
00:20:02,570 --> 00:20:04,797
There's no magnet: it's
an optical illusion.

296
00:20:04,837 --> 00:20:08,402
The surrounding slopes trick the eye
so a gentle descent looks uphill.

297
00:20:08,442 --> 00:20:10,205
Your brain is sure you're going up.

298
00:20:10,245 --> 00:20:11,647
The car honestly rolls down.

299
00:20:19,420 --> 00:20:21,487
Now, what I promised at the very start.

300
00:20:21,527 --> 00:20:23,270
The Himalayas, Uttarakhand state.

301
00:20:23,310 --> 00:20:26,133
About five thousand metres
up. Glacial lake Roopkund.

302
00:20:26,173 --> 00:20:28,348
Most of the year it's under ice and snow.

303
00:20:28,388 --> 00:20:32,453
When the ice melts, bones appear on the
shore and in the water. Human bones.

304
00:20:32,493 --> 00:20:34,290
The remains of hundreds of people.

305
00:20:40,170 --> 00:20:44,000
For a long time, everyone thought these
people died together, in one disaster.

306
00:20:44,040 --> 00:20:47,771
In two thousand nineteen, scientists
read the DNA of thirty-eight skeletons.

307
00:20:47,811 --> 00:20:50,103
And the story fell apart.
Not one group. Three.

308
00:20:50,143 --> 00:20:52,137
Twenty-three people were from South Asia.

309
00:20:52,177 --> 00:20:56,007
They died between the seventh and tenth
centuries, perhaps in different years.

310
00:20:56,047 --> 00:20:57,397
One was from Southeast Asia.

311
00:21:04,520 --> 00:21:06,670
And fourteen others
had... Mediterranean DNA.

312
00:21:06,710 --> 00:21:09,297
Closest to people living
today on Crete and in Greece.

313
00:21:09,337 --> 00:21:11,584
And they died around the
year eighteen hundred.

314
00:21:11,624 --> 00:21:13,190
A thousand years after the first.

315
00:21:13,230 --> 00:21:17,180
What were people from the Mediterranean
doing five kilometres up in the Himalayas?

316
00:21:17,220 --> 00:21:20,537
How did they get there? Why did they
all die? No answer. To this day.

317
00:21:29,940 --> 00:21:31,751
A quick technical stop. Still with us?

318
00:21:31,791 --> 00:21:34,673
Then you know about the Himalayan
skeletons, the pillar that

319
00:21:34,713 --> 00:21:37,108
won't rust, and the temple
carved from a mountain.

320
00:21:37,148 --> 00:21:38,374
Not bad for half a flight.

321
00:21:38,414 --> 00:21:41,539
If you like flying like this,
whole, no postcards, hit subscribe.

322
00:21:41,579 --> 00:21:44,315
Ahead: a marble wonder, a
quarter of the world economy...

323
00:21:44,355 --> 00:21:46,117
and the year the country tore in two.

324
00:21:59,140 --> 00:22:03,320
Around the year seventeen hundred, by
the estimate of economic historian Angus

325
00:22:03,360 --> 00:22:07,594
Maddison, India accounted for almost a
quarter of the world economy. A quarter.

326
00:22:07,634 --> 00:22:12,030
Empires followed one another here for
centuries: the Mauryas, the Guptas, who cast

327
00:22:12,070 --> 00:22:15,871
that iron pillar, the Delhi Sultanate,
the Great Mughals, the Marathas.

328
00:22:19,950 --> 00:22:23,167
The Mughals left the country's
most famous silhouette.

329
00:22:23,207 --> 00:22:25,700
The Taj Mahal isn't a palace. It's a tomb.

330
00:22:25,740 --> 00:22:29,560
Emperor Shah Jahan built it in
memory of his wife, Mumtaz Mahal.

331
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:31,188
It took about twenty years.

332
00:22:31,228 --> 00:22:34,987
White marble, with flowers of
semi-precious stones set by hand.

333
00:22:38,650 --> 00:22:40,310
The Taj had an older brother.

334
00:22:40,350 --> 00:22:44,297
Delhi's tomb of Emperor Humayun was
built about sixty years earlier.

335
00:22:44,337 --> 00:22:47,346
Same four-part gardens cut
by canals, same dome over

336
00:22:47,386 --> 00:22:49,633
the tomb, same idea: paradise on earth.

337
00:22:49,673 --> 00:22:51,333
The Taj grew largely from it.

338
00:22:51,373 --> 00:22:54,968
If the Taj Mahal is a poem,
Humayun's tomb is its first draft.

339
00:23:00,950 --> 00:23:04,288
And a stone's throw from this
marble lives everyday Agra.

340
00:23:04,328 --> 00:23:05,769
Cramped, noisy, not rich.

341
00:23:05,809 --> 00:23:10,510
Tourists barely see it: they're driven
from gate to gate. Remember this picture.

342
00:23:10,550 --> 00:23:12,643
We'll come back to that other India.

343
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:16,702
Then traders came by sea.

344
00:23:16,742 --> 00:23:19,848
Britain's East India Company
started with cloth and

345
00:23:19,888 --> 00:23:22,748
spices and ended up ruling
much of the country.

346
00:23:22,788 --> 00:23:27,190
From the mid-nineteenth century, the
British Crown ruled India directly.

347
00:23:28,940 --> 00:23:30,879
That era left some cheerful traces too.

348
00:23:30,919 --> 00:23:35,192
Jaipur, Rajasthan's capital, is called the
Pink City: in eighteen seventy-six its old

349
00:23:35,232 --> 00:23:38,744
centre was painted pink for the
British Prince of Wales, as a welcome.

350
00:23:38,784 --> 00:23:40,469
The guest left; the colour stayed.

351
00:23:40,509 --> 00:23:43,565
The Hawa Mahal, Palace of the
Winds: almost a thousand little

352
00:23:43,605 --> 00:23:46,305
windows, letting palace women
watch the street unseen.

353
00:23:53,080 --> 00:23:54,968
This is Calcutta, then
the capital, in eighteen

354
00:23:55,008 --> 00:23:57,143
ninety-nine: one of the
earliest films shot in India.

355
00:23:57,183 --> 00:23:59,112
And this is colour footage
from nineteen eleven.

356
00:23:59,152 --> 00:24:01,820
The Delhi Durbar: Britain's King
George the Fifth came to India in

357
00:24:01,860 --> 00:24:04,200
person, to appear before his
subjects as Emperor of India.

358
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:07,072
The same ceremony announced the
capital's move from Calcutta to Delhi.

359
00:24:07,112 --> 00:24:09,328
Elephants, uniforms, thousands
of people in the stands.

360
00:24:09,368 --> 00:24:11,175
An empire that thought
it would last forever.

361
00:24:11,215 --> 00:24:12,570
It had less than forty years left.

362
00:24:26,160 --> 00:24:28,480
By nineteen fifty,
India's share of the world

363
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:30,630
economy had fallen to about four percent.

364
00:24:30,670 --> 00:24:32,255
Almost a quarter, down to four.

365
00:24:32,295 --> 00:24:33,724
Why, historians still argue.

366
00:24:33,764 --> 00:24:36,450
Some say colonial rule
drained the country's wealth.

367
00:24:36,490 --> 00:24:39,125
Others say Europe had an
industrial revolution, and

368
00:24:39,165 --> 00:24:41,432
cheap factory cloth
crushed India's weavers.

369
00:24:41,472 --> 00:24:43,110
Most likely, both played a part.

370
00:24:52,420 --> 00:24:55,490
Meanwhile, a movement for independence
was rising against the empire.

371
00:24:55,530 --> 00:24:58,781
Its best-known face was Mahatma Gandhi,
a lawyer who swapped his suit for

372
00:24:58,821 --> 00:25:01,351
simple cloth and proposed a
strange weapon: non-violence.

373
00:25:01,391 --> 00:25:04,055
In nineteen thirty, he led
people on foot to the sea to make

374
00:25:04,095 --> 00:25:07,211
their own salt, defying the British
salt tax. It looked like a trifle.

375
00:25:07,251 --> 00:25:08,338
It became a world symbol.

376
00:25:18,140 --> 00:25:20,378
In nineteen forty-three,
famine struck Bengal.

377
00:25:20,418 --> 00:25:24,290
Estimates of the dead range from eight
hundred thousand to almost four million.

378
00:25:24,330 --> 00:25:26,172
Most often cited: about three million.

379
00:25:26,212 --> 00:25:29,441
It was wartime, rice from neighbouring
Burma stopped arriving, and

380
00:25:29,481 --> 00:25:32,709
historians still argue how many
lives could have been saved if the

381
00:25:32,749 --> 00:25:35,235
British colonial authorities
had acted differently.

382
00:25:40,090 --> 00:25:43,447
On the fifteenth of August, nineteen
forty-seven, India became independent.

383
00:25:43,487 --> 00:25:46,481
But in those same days, the map
was cut in two: India and Pakistan.

384
00:25:46,521 --> 00:25:48,383
One of history's largest migrations began.

385
00:25:48,423 --> 00:25:51,825
Twelve to twenty million people left
their homes and crossed the new border.

386
00:25:51,865 --> 00:25:54,542
In both directions. Trains ran
packed, people rode on roofs.

387
00:25:54,582 --> 00:25:56,807
In the violence of those
months, estimates say two

388
00:25:56,847 --> 00:25:58,800
hundred thousand to two
million people died.

389
00:26:08,290 --> 00:26:10,198
Behind the numbers, simple stories.

390
00:26:10,238 --> 00:26:14,205
Neighbours on one street yesterday,
on opposite sides of a border today.

391
00:26:14,245 --> 00:26:17,934
Families who lost each other at a
station and searched for decades.

392
00:26:17,974 --> 00:26:22,275
In India and Pakistan, some people still
remember that road. Fewer every year.

393
00:26:22,315 --> 00:26:23,555
But the border remains.

394
00:26:27,570 --> 00:26:31,470
At midnight on the fifteenth of August, the
first prime minister, Jawaharlal Nehru, told

395
00:26:31,510 --> 00:26:35,366
the Constituent Assembly words India knows
by heart: 'When the world sleeps, India will

396
00:26:35,406 --> 00:26:39,262
awake to life and freedom.' A country where
most people then couldn't read started from

397
00:26:39,302 --> 00:26:41,948
zero, with all its languages,
religions, castes and poverty.

398
00:26:41,988 --> 00:26:44,724
Many in the West doubted it would
even hold together. It held.

399
00:26:54,380 --> 00:26:58,105
In January nineteen fifty, the new
republic's constitution came into force.

400
00:26:58,145 --> 00:27:00,715
Its chief architect was the
lawyer Bhimrao Ambedkar,

401
00:27:00,755 --> 00:27:02,824
himself born into an 'untouchable' family.

402
00:27:02,864 --> 00:27:06,438
And the constitution has Article
Seventeen: untouchability is abolished.

403
00:27:06,478 --> 00:27:09,400
A thousand-year-old order,
cancelled in one line. On paper.

404
00:27:09,440 --> 00:27:12,713
How far that line became life,
we'll look at honestly a bit later.

405
00:27:17,690 --> 00:27:20,008
Empires came and went. Flags changed.

406
00:27:20,048 --> 00:27:23,895
But one thing in India hasn't
changed for thousands of years.

407
00:27:23,935 --> 00:27:26,763
Every morning, people
walk down to the river.

408
00:27:29,930 --> 00:27:31,647
Remember the two rivers meeting?

409
00:27:31,687 --> 00:27:33,513
Here it gets its name: the Ganges.

410
00:27:33,553 --> 00:27:37,191
Then about two and a half thousand
kilometres to the Bay of Bengal.

411
00:27:37,231 --> 00:27:41,418
For hundreds of millions of Hindus, it's
no mere river, but a goddess. Ganga.

412
00:27:45,970 --> 00:27:47,609
Its most famous city: Varanasi.

413
00:27:47,649 --> 00:27:50,642
One of the world's oldest
continuously inhabited cities.

414
00:27:50,682 --> 00:27:54,758
Kilometres of ghats line the bank, stone
steps leading right into the water.

415
00:27:54,798 --> 00:27:57,628
At dawn, people wade in to
pray, bathe, wash clothes.

416
00:27:57,668 --> 00:28:00,986
In the evening comes the aarti,
a fire ceremony for the river.

417
00:28:01,026 --> 00:28:03,368
Flames, bells, song,
thousands on the steps.

418
00:28:09,040 --> 00:28:10,762
Varanasi is best seen from a boat.

419
00:28:10,802 --> 00:28:14,544
You drift slowly along the bank, and
the city unrolls like a long scroll.

420
00:28:14,584 --> 00:28:15,840
Boys jump into the water.

421
00:28:15,880 --> 00:28:17,342
A woman washes a bright sari.

422
00:28:17,382 --> 00:28:21,177
A sadhu in orange, so still he seems
to have sat there since last century.

423
00:28:21,217 --> 00:28:25,529
Goats chew flower garlands someone offered
the river this morning. All in one frame.

424
00:28:34,560 --> 00:28:38,651
Upstream, where the Ganges leaves the
mountains, the water is cold and clear.

425
00:28:38,691 --> 00:28:42,836
In Rishikesh and Haridwar, the evening
fire ceremony happens above the rapids.

426
00:28:42,876 --> 00:28:44,982
The world comes here for yoga and quiet.

427
00:28:45,022 --> 00:28:48,147
Here it's easiest to see why
they call the river mother: it

428
00:28:48,187 --> 00:28:50,615
comes from sky and
glaciers to feed the plain.

429
00:28:56,510 --> 00:28:59,714
On one ghat, the fire never
goes out. A cremation ground.

430
00:28:59,754 --> 00:29:01,137
Pyres burn day and night.

431
00:29:01,177 --> 00:29:04,723
For a devout Hindu, to die in
Varanasi and pass into the Ganges

432
00:29:04,763 --> 00:29:07,455
means release from the
endless cycle of rebirth.

433
00:29:07,495 --> 00:29:09,276
From outside, it can seem heavy.

434
00:29:09,316 --> 00:29:12,179
For a believer, it's the
brightest end of the road.

435
00:29:18,090 --> 00:29:21,520
Now picture the biggest gathering of
people you can imagine. And multiply it.

436
00:29:21,560 --> 00:29:24,901
In early two thousand twenty-five, in
Prayagraj, where the Ganges meets the

437
00:29:24,941 --> 00:29:27,830
Yamuna, the Maha Kumbh took place,
the greatest bathing festival.

438
00:29:27,870 --> 00:29:30,038
Over forty-five days, the
authorities counted six

439
00:29:30,078 --> 00:29:31,706
hundred and sixty-two million visits.

440
00:29:31,746 --> 00:29:33,734
Not people, visits: many
came more than once.

441
00:29:33,774 --> 00:29:36,979
Even so, that's more than the populations
of the EU and Russia combined.

442
00:29:46,770 --> 00:29:48,038
It's visible from space.

443
00:29:48,078 --> 00:29:51,689
A satellite image: dark land, and
a tent city glowing in the night.

444
00:29:54,810 --> 00:29:58,197
Why here? Legend says when gods and
demons fought over the nectar of

445
00:29:58,237 --> 00:30:00,969
immortality, a few drops fell
to earth, in four places.

446
00:30:01,009 --> 00:30:02,783
One is this confluence at Prayagraj.

447
00:30:02,823 --> 00:30:05,555
Believers come to wash away
their sins, on special days

448
00:30:05,595 --> 00:30:07,621
set by the stars and an ancient calendar.

449
00:30:07,661 --> 00:30:10,242
First into the water go the
sadhus, hermits, many of

450
00:30:10,282 --> 00:30:12,157
whom gave up everything, even clothes.

451
00:30:21,270 --> 00:30:24,007
On the twenty-ninth of
January, one of the main

452
00:30:24,047 --> 00:30:26,665
bathing days, a crush
broke out in the crowd.

453
00:30:26,705 --> 00:30:28,851
Officially, thirty-seven people died.

454
00:30:28,891 --> 00:30:31,451
BBC journalists counted
at least eighty-two.

455
00:30:33,470 --> 00:30:35,989
And a question that's awkward
to ask. What's the water like?

456
00:30:36,029 --> 00:30:39,358
In mid-January, state environmental
officials took samples at the bathing site.

457
00:30:39,398 --> 00:30:42,088
Faecal bacteria, the kind that
come from human and animal waste,

458
00:30:42,128 --> 00:30:44,348
measured forty-nine thousand
per hundred millilitres.

459
00:30:44,388 --> 00:30:46,267
The bathing limit is
two and a half thousand.

460
00:30:46,307 --> 00:30:47,333
Almost twenty times over.

461
00:30:47,373 --> 00:30:49,423
A month later, the same
agency recalculated using

462
00:30:49,463 --> 00:30:51,470
averages and declared the
water fit for bathing.

463
00:30:51,510 --> 00:30:52,579
Both figures are official.

464
00:31:06,770 --> 00:31:09,534
Why? It's not that people
don't love their river.

465
00:31:09,574 --> 00:31:12,452
Few rivers on Earth are
loved more. It's the pipes.

466
00:31:12,492 --> 00:31:16,572
India's cities produce about seventy-two
billion litres of sewage a day.

467
00:31:16,612 --> 00:31:18,231
Less than a third is treated.

468
00:31:18,271 --> 00:31:20,635
The rest seeps into the ground and rivers.

469
00:31:24,940 --> 00:31:27,864
Perhaps this film's most
incredible image. That's not snow.

470
00:31:27,904 --> 00:31:29,673
It's the Yamuna in Delhi, in autumn.

471
00:31:29,713 --> 00:31:33,039
The foam comes from phosphates in
washing powder and factory waste.

472
00:31:33,079 --> 00:31:36,958
People stand waist-deep in it, praying
to the rising sun: the Chhath festival.

473
00:31:36,998 --> 00:31:39,119
Doctors warn the foam
harms skin and lungs.

474
00:31:39,159 --> 00:31:41,078
But faith here is older than factories.

475
00:31:41,118 --> 00:31:42,535
And it won't yield the river.

476
00:31:50,250 --> 00:31:52,586
See that mountain on Delhi's
horizon? Not the Himalayas.

477
00:31:52,626 --> 00:31:53,732
It's the Ghazipur landfill.

478
00:31:53,772 --> 00:31:55,174
It opened in nineteen eighty-four.

479
00:31:55,214 --> 00:31:56,532
By two thousand two it was full.

480
00:31:56,572 --> 00:31:58,653
In two thousand twenty-two,
drone footage showed a

481
00:31:58,693 --> 00:32:00,096
height of about sixty-five metres.

482
00:32:00,136 --> 00:32:02,345
For comparison, the Taj
Mahal is about seventy-three.

483
00:32:02,385 --> 00:32:05,230
In two thousand seventeen, part of
it collapsed, killing two people.

484
00:32:05,270 --> 00:32:07,648
Now it's being dismantled,
processed layer by layer, with

485
00:32:07,688 --> 00:32:09,940
completion promised by late
two thousand twenty-seven.

486
00:32:21,330 --> 00:32:23,655
People live beside such mountains too.

487
00:32:23,695 --> 00:32:27,576
Homes stand on the edge of another
huge dump, Deonar in Mumbai.

488
00:32:27,616 --> 00:32:32,555
Children walk past smouldering slopes to
school, adults gather what can be sold.

489
00:32:32,595 --> 00:32:36,911
In two thousand sixteen, Deonar's
fire sent smoke over the whole city.

490
00:32:39,540 --> 00:32:41,170
Rubbish reaches the sea too.

491
00:32:41,210 --> 00:32:44,750
After big festivals and memorial
rites, Mumbai's beaches are

492
00:32:44,790 --> 00:32:47,434
left with piles of
plastic, flowers and bags.

493
00:32:47,474 --> 00:32:50,536
Each time, volunteers turn
out: hundreds with gloves

494
00:32:50,576 --> 00:32:52,803
and sacks, clearing the shore by hand.

495
00:32:52,843 --> 00:32:54,533
To start again a month later.

496
00:32:57,890 --> 00:33:00,288
But the Ganges isn't the
country's only holy place.

497
00:33:00,328 --> 00:33:01,914
And Hinduism isn't its only faith.

498
00:33:01,954 --> 00:33:04,687
By the last census, about eight
in ten Indians are Hindus.

499
00:33:04,727 --> 00:33:08,225
But one in seven is Muslim: one of the
world's largest Muslim communities.

500
00:33:08,265 --> 00:33:10,186
Plus Christians, Sikhs, Buddhists, Jains.

501
00:33:10,226 --> 00:33:13,772
A mosque, temple and gurdwara can share
one street, and have for centuries.

502
00:33:13,812 --> 00:33:15,541
Not always peacefully. But they live.

503
00:33:24,720 --> 00:33:27,462
In Kolkata, on a branch
of the Ganges, the goddess

504
00:33:27,502 --> 00:33:29,799
Durga is sent into the river every autumn.

505
00:33:29,839 --> 00:33:33,749
Huge painted statues parade to drums,
and the city stays up for nights.

506
00:33:33,789 --> 00:33:36,865
Durga Puja is even on UNESCO's
intangible heritage list.

507
00:33:36,905 --> 00:33:38,645
Here the river isn't a backdrop.

508
00:33:38,685 --> 00:33:40,426
It's the festival's main player.

509
00:33:45,990 --> 00:33:48,169
Here's a place where faith
looks quite different.

510
00:33:48,209 --> 00:33:50,026
Amritsar, the Golden Temple of the Sikhs.

511
00:33:50,066 --> 00:33:51,158
Here, everyone eats free.

512
00:33:51,198 --> 00:33:52,788
Any faith, any caste, rich and poor.

513
00:33:52,828 --> 00:33:55,188
Everyone sits on the floor,
side by side, in one row.

514
00:33:55,228 --> 00:33:58,358
Estimates: forty to a hundred thousand
people a day, more on holidays.

515
00:33:58,398 --> 00:34:02,117
Volunteers do most of the cooking, serving
and washing up. Nobody asks who you are.

516
00:34:02,157 --> 00:34:02,886
Just sit and eat.

517
00:34:14,210 --> 00:34:17,445
A river they can't yet clean, and a
kitchen where nobody asks who you are.

518
00:34:17,485 --> 00:34:21,075
Both are India. But to understand it
honestly, standing by the water isn't enough.

519
00:34:21,115 --> 00:34:24,218
We need the numbers that tourist
brochures leave out. It won't be easy.

520
00:34:24,258 --> 00:34:26,918
But among them is one that truly
surprised me. In a good way.

521
00:34:26,958 --> 00:34:30,591
And remember: behind every number are
people, and almost every number has a reason.

522
00:34:43,730 --> 00:34:48,217
Remember the wall from chapter one?
The Himalayas. Here it becomes a trap.

523
00:34:48,257 --> 00:34:52,132
In winter over northern India,
cold air hugs the ground, there's

524
00:34:52,172 --> 00:34:55,681
almost no wind, and the mountains
to the north hold it in.

525
00:34:55,721 --> 00:34:58,495
Whatever enters that air
hangs over the plain.

526
00:34:58,535 --> 00:35:03,695
And plenty does: stubble fires in Punjab
and Haryana, coal power plants, car exhaust,

527
00:35:03,735 --> 00:35:07,916
construction dust, and smoke from
wood and dried dung in home stoves.

528
00:35:12,390 --> 00:35:15,232
According to two thousand
twenty-five data, five of the

529
00:35:15,272 --> 00:35:17,538
world's ten most polluted
cities are Indian.

530
00:35:17,578 --> 00:35:19,267
Number one: Loni, a Delhi suburb.

531
00:35:19,307 --> 00:35:22,097
And Delhi itself is the
world's most polluted capital.

532
00:35:22,137 --> 00:35:24,769
Its air holds sixteen times
more fine dust than the

533
00:35:24,809 --> 00:35:26,708
World Health Organization deems safe.

534
00:35:26,748 --> 00:35:28,071
That's the yearly average.

535
00:35:28,111 --> 00:35:29,381
Winter can be much worse.

536
00:35:34,910 --> 00:35:37,824
The Lancet estimates dirty
air kills about one point

537
00:35:37,864 --> 00:35:41,345
seven million people a year in
India. More than all of Munich.

538
00:35:41,385 --> 00:35:45,094
It's a model, not a census, and
the ministry disputes such models.

539
00:35:45,134 --> 00:35:47,934
But even cautious estimates
agree: the air here is

540
00:35:47,974 --> 00:35:50,036
one of the top causes of early death.

541
00:35:55,450 --> 00:35:59,731
Next, where people live. The last
census, in two thousand eleven, counted

542
00:35:59,771 --> 00:36:02,158
about sixty-five million people in slums.

543
00:36:02,198 --> 00:36:04,111
Roughly one in six city dwellers.

544
00:36:04,151 --> 00:36:06,716
In Mumbai, over forty
percent of households.

545
00:36:06,756 --> 00:36:09,853
No newer data: the next
census is only happening now.

546
00:36:12,500 --> 00:36:14,970
Dharavi is one of Asia's best-known slums.

547
00:36:15,010 --> 00:36:18,495
About two square kilometres,
holding seven hundred thousand

548
00:36:18,535 --> 00:36:21,124
to a million people,
by different estimates.

549
00:36:21,164 --> 00:36:24,411
But look closer: it's not
just poverty. It's workshops.

550
00:36:24,451 --> 00:36:28,115
Potters, sewing shops, recycling
plastic from all over Mumbai.

551
00:36:28,155 --> 00:36:32,477
Dharavi is a giant factory where almost
everyone makes something by hand.

552
00:36:32,517 --> 00:36:33,971
A huge rebuild has begun.

553
00:36:34,011 --> 00:36:38,512
New homes are promised, but residents
argue over where those who don't get a

554
00:36:38,552 --> 00:36:42,217
flat will be sent: some land was
set aside beside an old dump.

555
00:36:47,020 --> 00:36:50,058
Remember the Agra behind
the Taj Mahal? Here it is.

556
00:36:50,098 --> 00:36:53,195
Narrow streets, open drains,
children playing by the

557
00:36:53,235 --> 00:36:55,368
water, shops the size of a wardrobe.

558
00:36:55,408 --> 00:36:57,902
Minutes from one of the New Seven Wonders.

559
00:36:57,942 --> 00:37:00,557
Tourists rarely come
here. People just live.

560
00:37:04,100 --> 00:37:07,009
Who cleans up after a country
of one and a half billion?

561
00:37:07,049 --> 00:37:08,693
Largely, informal waste pickers.

562
00:37:08,733 --> 00:37:11,326
They sort rubbish by hand,
pull out plastic, metal

563
00:37:11,366 --> 00:37:13,274
and glass, and sell it for recycling.

564
00:37:13,314 --> 00:37:15,275
Without this work, cities would choke.

565
00:37:15,315 --> 00:37:18,908
Yet there's little thanks: many
pickers say people look down on them.

566
00:37:24,460 --> 00:37:26,718
An awkward subject, so
rarely discussed. Toilets.

567
00:37:26,758 --> 00:37:29,767
Since two thousand fourteen, the
country has built over a hundred

568
00:37:29,807 --> 00:37:32,582
million household toilets, now
about one hundred and twenty.

569
00:37:32,622 --> 00:37:36,334
In two thousand nineteen, every village
declared itself free of open defecation.

570
00:37:36,374 --> 00:37:40,274
Declared it. Themselves. But a big national
survey from those years found almost one

571
00:37:40,314 --> 00:37:42,432
household in five still
used no toilet at all.

572
00:37:49,800 --> 00:37:53,322
Why? Because building a toilet
isn't enough. It needs water.

573
00:37:53,362 --> 00:37:54,806
A pit someone will empty.

574
00:37:54,846 --> 00:37:59,021
And a change to a centuries-old habit,
which takes longer than digging.

575
00:37:59,061 --> 00:38:02,286
In villages without toilets,
women still wait for dark.

576
00:38:05,660 --> 00:38:07,146
Who cleans the pits and sewers?

577
00:38:07,186 --> 00:38:10,985
Unprotected sewer entry has been illegal
in India since two thousand thirteen.

578
00:38:11,025 --> 00:38:15,020
Official figures alone record three hundred
and seventy-seven deaths in sewers and

579
00:38:15,060 --> 00:38:18,416
septic tanks from two thousand
nineteen to two thousand twenty-three.

580
00:38:18,456 --> 00:38:21,861
Activists say more. Over nine in ten
of these workers come from castes

581
00:38:21,901 --> 00:38:23,731
and communities long seen as 'lowest'.

582
00:38:29,850 --> 00:38:32,476
Here's the promised return to
that line in the constitution.

583
00:38:32,516 --> 00:38:34,786
Untouchability has been
banned since nineteen fifty.

584
00:38:34,826 --> 00:38:36,874
Former 'untouchables'
and tribes have quotas in

585
00:38:36,914 --> 00:38:38,874
universities, government
jobs and parliament.

586
00:38:38,914 --> 00:38:42,428
But in two thousand twenty-four, police
registered more than fifty-five thousand

587
00:38:42,468 --> 00:38:45,539
crimes against Dalits, as former
'untouchables' call themselves today.

588
00:38:45,579 --> 00:38:46,960
About a hundred and fifty a day.

589
00:38:47,000 --> 00:38:48,293
The line was written in a day.

590
00:38:48,333 --> 00:38:49,448
Life changes over decades.

591
00:38:58,700 --> 00:39:01,738
What comes next is mixed: hard in
places, surprisingly good in others.

592
00:39:01,778 --> 00:39:04,113
I'll tell you which way each
number is moving. School.

593
00:39:04,153 --> 00:39:06,620
The good news: literacy rose
from seventy-four percent in

594
00:39:06,660 --> 00:39:08,423
two thousand eleven to almost eighty-one.

595
00:39:08,463 --> 00:39:09,654
In Kerala, over ninety-five.

596
00:39:09,694 --> 00:39:12,600
Now the bad: being able to read
isn't the same as understanding it.

597
00:39:12,640 --> 00:39:14,711
A big study of rural
schools found about half of

598
00:39:14,751 --> 00:39:16,998
fifth-graders can't read a
simple second-grade text.

599
00:39:28,950 --> 00:39:31,292
Here's the key thing:
there are many Indias.

600
00:39:31,332 --> 00:39:34,594
Inside one country, gaps can be
as wide as between countries.

601
00:39:34,634 --> 00:39:37,192
In Kerala and Mizoram,
almost everyone can read.

602
00:39:37,232 --> 00:39:39,195
Other states are still far from that.

603
00:39:39,235 --> 00:39:41,902
So India's average says
little: some places are at

604
00:39:41,942 --> 00:39:44,392
European levels, others
are just starting out.

605
00:39:49,620 --> 00:39:52,662
Now, children. Here the numbers
are falling, but slowly.

606
00:39:52,702 --> 00:39:57,231
Child marriage: ten years ago, one young
woman in four was married before eighteen.

607
00:39:57,271 --> 00:39:58,992
The new survey says one in five.

608
00:39:59,032 --> 00:40:02,625
Almost one child in three under
five is stunted by undernutrition.

609
00:40:02,665 --> 00:40:04,826
It was over a third; that's still a lot.

610
00:40:11,190 --> 00:40:13,440
Here, though, the numbers fall steadily.

611
00:40:13,480 --> 00:40:17,047
India has about a quarter of the
world's tuberculosis patients.

612
00:40:17,087 --> 00:40:20,539
Yet since two thousand fifteen,
its incidence fell twenty-one

613
00:40:20,579 --> 00:40:23,516
percent, almost twice as
fast as the global average.

614
00:40:23,556 --> 00:40:27,638
Malaria: from two thousand seventeen
to two thousand twenty-three, cases

615
00:40:27,678 --> 00:40:30,787
fell more than threefold,
from over six million to two.

616
00:40:30,827 --> 00:40:35,081
Slowest to retreat is rabies: the World
Health Organization estimates India

617
00:40:35,121 --> 00:40:38,402
accounts for about a third of
all rabies deaths worldwide.

618
00:40:38,442 --> 00:40:40,348
The main cause is stray dog bites.

619
00:40:45,100 --> 00:40:48,139
Now, a sight that surprises every
tourist: a cow standing on the highway.

620
00:40:48,179 --> 00:40:50,079
You glimpsed it at the
start. Why is it there?

621
00:40:50,119 --> 00:40:53,369
India has almost two hundred million
cattle, about five million of them stray.

622
00:40:53,409 --> 00:40:56,069
Hindus hold the cow sacred, and
most states ban slaughtering it.

623
00:40:56,109 --> 00:40:57,840
An old cow without milk is costly to feed.

624
00:40:57,880 --> 00:41:00,750
And selling it is now nearly
impossible: after attacks by self-styled

625
00:41:00,790 --> 00:41:03,281
'cow protectors', the cattle
trade collapsed in many places.

626
00:41:03,321 --> 00:41:04,715
So the farmer just opens the gate.

627
00:41:19,470 --> 00:41:21,855
But the cow is hardly
the road's main danger.

628
00:41:21,895 --> 00:41:24,549
In two thousand twenty-four,
about one hundred and

629
00:41:24,589 --> 00:41:28,214
seventy-seven thousand people died
on India's roads. Twenty an hour.

630
00:41:28,254 --> 00:41:29,884
Almost half were motorcyclists.

631
00:41:29,924 --> 00:41:33,602
The year before, more than fifty-four
thousand died without a helmet.

632
00:41:33,642 --> 00:41:35,974
And seven deaths in ten
are due to speeding.

633
00:41:40,520 --> 00:41:44,301
Women's safety. In two thousand
twenty-four, police registered four hundred

634
00:41:44,341 --> 00:41:46,543
and forty-one thousand
crimes against women.

635
00:41:46,583 --> 00:41:47,868
Over twelve hundred a day.

636
00:41:47,908 --> 00:41:50,824
And the largest category isn't
attacks by strangers in the

637
00:41:50,864 --> 00:41:53,422
street, but cruelty by a
husband and his relatives.

638
00:41:53,462 --> 00:41:57,651
That's only what was reported: researchers
are sure the real number is much higher.

639
00:42:03,850 --> 00:42:05,481
But there's a counter-trend.

640
00:42:05,521 --> 00:42:09,301
The share of women working or
looking for work almost doubled in

641
00:42:09,341 --> 00:42:12,345
six years, from twenty-three
percent to over forty.

642
00:42:12,385 --> 00:42:15,866
Much of it is family work,
often unpaid. But it's movement.

643
00:42:19,020 --> 00:42:21,690
And now the promised number
that truly surprised me.

644
00:42:21,730 --> 00:42:24,399
According to the World Bank,
in two thousand eleven,

645
00:42:24,439 --> 00:42:27,369
twenty-seven percent of Indians
lived in extreme poverty.

646
00:42:27,409 --> 00:42:29,402
By two thousand twenty-two, about five.

647
00:42:29,442 --> 00:42:32,059
In eleven years, roughly
two hundred and sixty-nine

648
00:42:32,099 --> 00:42:33,883
million people escaped destitution.

649
00:42:33,923 --> 00:42:36,436
That's more than the entire
population of Brazil.

650
00:42:40,520 --> 00:42:43,173
But five percent of one
and a half billion is still

651
00:42:43,213 --> 00:42:46,235
about seventy-five million
people. More than the whole UK.

652
00:42:46,275 --> 00:42:47,818
Wealth is split very unevenly.

653
00:42:47,858 --> 00:42:50,616
The richest one percent earn
over a fifth of national

654
00:42:50,656 --> 00:42:52,675
income, higher than under British rule.

655
00:42:52,715 --> 00:42:55,632
That one percent owns about
forty percent of all wealth.

656
00:43:01,440 --> 00:43:04,352
These seventy-five million
don't hide. You see them.

657
00:43:04,392 --> 00:43:09,007
People sleep under flyovers, beg at stations,
children sell flowers at red lights.

658
00:43:09,047 --> 00:43:11,902
It's not a reason to look
away, nor for showy pity.

659
00:43:11,942 --> 00:43:14,910
Just part of the same city
as the skyscrapers behind.

660
00:43:18,790 --> 00:43:22,523
Tough chapter. But almost every
hardship here has a brighter number too.

661
00:43:22,563 --> 00:43:26,821
A country of one and a half billion has
problems sized for one and a half billion.

662
00:43:26,861 --> 00:43:29,232
And now, the same country
from the other side.

663
00:43:29,272 --> 00:43:30,856
The side it's rightly proud of.

664
00:43:30,896 --> 00:43:32,324
And we'll start on the Moon.

665
00:43:39,320 --> 00:43:41,314
Remember the craft from the opening?

666
00:43:41,354 --> 00:43:44,195
August the twenty-third,
two thousand twenty-three.

667
00:43:44,235 --> 00:43:47,698
India's Vikram lander touches
down near the Moon's south pole.

668
00:43:47,738 --> 00:43:50,297
No one had landed so
close to the pole before.

669
00:43:50,337 --> 00:43:53,518
India became the fourth country
to soft-land on the Moon.

670
00:43:56,690 --> 00:44:00,391
Look at these people. Mission
control, at the moment of landing.

671
00:44:00,431 --> 00:44:04,307
Now, the whole mission's price:
about seventy-five million dollars.

672
00:44:04,347 --> 00:44:07,814
The film Interstellar cost about
one hundred and sixty-five.

673
00:44:07,854 --> 00:44:10,970
A real Moon landing cost
less than half a space movie.

674
00:44:13,860 --> 00:44:14,620
It wasn't luck.

675
00:44:14,660 --> 00:44:18,515
In two thousand fourteen, an Indian
probe reached Mars, on the first try.

676
00:44:18,555 --> 00:44:20,489
It cost seventy-four million dollars.

677
00:44:20,529 --> 00:44:22,090
Cheaper than the film Gravity.

678
00:44:22,130 --> 00:44:24,704
The American probe arriving
two days earlier cost

679
00:44:24,744 --> 00:44:26,624
six hundred and seventy-one million.

680
00:44:26,664 --> 00:44:28,598
India's was meant to work six months.

681
00:44:28,638 --> 00:44:30,092
It worked about eight years.

682
00:44:35,190 --> 00:44:38,597
India is now building its own
spacecraft for astronauts.

683
00:44:38,637 --> 00:44:42,597
The first crewed flight is planned
for two thousand twenty-seven.

684
00:44:42,637 --> 00:44:45,059
Dates keep slipping, so fingers crossed.

685
00:44:45,680 --> 00:44:48,506
One more image from space.
This is India at night.

686
00:44:48,546 --> 00:44:52,403
Compare images a decade apart, and
you see the country brighten: new

687
00:44:52,443 --> 00:44:55,441
lights flare up, cities
grow, power reaches villages.

688
00:44:55,481 --> 00:44:58,422
It's perhaps the clearest
economic chart you can see

689
00:44:58,462 --> 00:45:00,543
without opening a single spreadsheet.

690
00:45:04,030 --> 00:45:06,025
From orbit to the street.
This man sells tea.

691
00:45:06,065 --> 00:45:07,246
A small glass, fire, steam.

692
00:45:07,286 --> 00:45:09,145
On his cart hangs a little square of code.

693
00:45:09,185 --> 00:45:11,813
A customer aims a phone, and
the money arrives in a second.

694
00:45:11,853 --> 00:45:14,210
It's India's instant payment
system, named on screen.

695
00:45:14,250 --> 00:45:16,561
It now handles over twenty
billion payments a month.

696
00:45:16,601 --> 00:45:19,365
In two thousand twenty-three,
by one estimate, almost half the

697
00:45:19,405 --> 00:45:21,716
world's instant digital
payments went through India.

698
00:45:32,340 --> 00:45:34,903
Bengaluru is called
India's Silicon Valley.

699
00:45:34,943 --> 00:45:38,233
The country's IT industry
earns more than three hundred

700
00:45:38,273 --> 00:45:41,079
billion dollars a year,
most of it from abroad.

701
00:45:41,119 --> 00:45:45,438
And Google and Microsoft are now run
by people born and raised in India.

702
00:45:48,860 --> 00:45:51,596
India's economy is now
the world's sixth largest.

703
00:45:51,636 --> 00:45:56,071
The IMF expects it to pass Japan in two
thousand twenty-seven, becoming fourth.

704
00:45:56,111 --> 00:45:57,884
Another record, far from cosmic.

705
00:45:57,924 --> 00:46:01,509
India is the world's top milk
producer, about one litre in four.

706
00:46:01,549 --> 00:46:05,984
Much of it from village families with a
few cows or buffaloes, not giant farms.

707
00:46:10,870 --> 00:46:12,572
India without tea? Unthinkable.

708
00:46:12,612 --> 00:46:14,708
It's one of the world's top producers.

709
00:46:14,748 --> 00:46:18,024
Darjeeling on the Himalayan
slopes, Assam in the northeast,

710
00:46:18,064 --> 00:46:21,003
Nilgiri in the south: names
you know from the packet.

711
00:46:21,043 --> 00:46:24,094
Locals drink it their way:
with milk, sugar and spices,

712
00:46:24,134 --> 00:46:26,230
in small glasses, several times a day.

713
00:46:30,220 --> 00:46:33,574
Indian Railways carry over
seven billion passengers a year.

714
00:46:33,614 --> 00:46:35,529
About twenty million people a day.

715
00:46:35,569 --> 00:46:38,923
As if all of greater New York
boarded a train each morning.

716
00:46:38,963 --> 00:46:42,202
In Mumbai's rush-hour suburban
trains, people ride in the

717
00:46:42,242 --> 00:46:44,733
open doors, holding the
rail and each other.

718
00:46:48,550 --> 00:46:50,933
There are cities where
time seems slower. Kolkata.

719
00:46:50,973 --> 00:46:54,228
Old-model yellow taxis that elsewhere
became museum pieces long ago.

720
00:46:54,268 --> 00:46:57,814
An old tram, a kind almost gone from
India, ringing its bell on the bends.

721
00:46:57,854 --> 00:47:00,479
The huge steel Howrah Bridge
over the river, carrying a

722
00:47:00,519 --> 00:47:02,320
daily flood of people, cars and carts.

723
00:47:02,360 --> 00:47:04,889
A city that was the capital
of British India, and now

724
00:47:04,929 --> 00:47:06,730
just lives, noisily and with pleasure.

725
00:47:16,890 --> 00:47:19,820
Delhi's metro runs
underground and over rooftops:

726
00:47:19,860 --> 00:47:22,548
clean, air-conditioned,
with women-only cars.

727
00:47:22,588 --> 00:47:25,822
Its first line opened only
in the early two thousands.

728
00:47:25,862 --> 00:47:29,762
In the Thar, where caravans once
passed, wind turbines now stand.

729
00:47:29,802 --> 00:47:32,065
New India is built right over the old.

730
00:47:33,920 --> 00:47:35,674
How many languages does India speak?

731
00:47:35,714 --> 00:47:38,314
The two thousand eleven
census recorded over nineteen

732
00:47:38,354 --> 00:47:40,307
and a half thousand mother-tongue names.

733
00:47:40,347 --> 00:47:42,997
Linguists grouped them into
one hundred and twenty-one

734
00:47:43,037 --> 00:47:45,837
languages, and twenty-two are
listed in the constitution.

735
00:47:45,877 --> 00:47:49,923
Ride a train overnight and wake up where
people speak and write quite differently.

736
00:47:54,350 --> 00:47:56,831
In these languages, they
make films. Lots of films.

737
00:47:56,871 --> 00:48:01,131
India is one of the world's great film nations,
and Bollywood in Mumbai is only part of

738
00:48:01,171 --> 00:48:04,640
it: there are big film industries in
Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Bengali.

739
00:48:04,680 --> 00:48:07,557
Here's Mumbai's Film City
studio: a station set where films

740
00:48:07,597 --> 00:48:09,633
loved by generations of Indians were shot.

741
00:48:09,673 --> 00:48:12,895
Nobody here is surprised when,
mid-scene, everyone starts dancing.

742
00:48:20,430 --> 00:48:21,214
Of course, food.

743
00:48:21,254 --> 00:48:25,284
In this Old Delhi spice market, pepper
stings your eyes and even sellers cough.

744
00:48:25,324 --> 00:48:28,067
But guess what wasn't here
five centuries ago? Chilli.

745
00:48:28,107 --> 00:48:31,622
The Portuguese brought it from the
Americas in the sixteenth century.

746
00:48:31,662 --> 00:48:33,682
Before that, black pepper gave the heat.

747
00:48:33,722 --> 00:48:36,671
Picture Indian food without
chilli. For millennia, it was.

748
00:48:42,180 --> 00:48:44,942
Lunch is its own ritual, and
less simple than it looks.

749
00:48:44,982 --> 00:48:48,051
A thali, a big tray of little
bowls: rice, flatbread, several

750
00:48:48,091 --> 00:48:50,751
vegetables, lentils, something
sour, something sweet.

751
00:48:50,791 --> 00:48:52,789
It's often eaten by hand, the right one.

752
00:48:52,829 --> 00:48:55,541
They say it tastes better:
your fingers feel it first.

753
00:48:55,581 --> 00:48:57,732
Haven't tried it
myself... but I'd love to.

754
00:49:04,700 --> 00:49:07,218
In spring, the country
explodes with colour.

755
00:49:07,258 --> 00:49:10,357
Holi, the spring festival
when you can douse anyone in

756
00:49:10,397 --> 00:49:14,832
powder: a neighbour, the boss, a stranger.
Nobody minds. Well, almost nobody.

757
00:49:17,020 --> 00:49:19,649
In autumn comes Diwali,
the festival of lights.

758
00:49:19,689 --> 00:49:23,339
Millions of little clay lamps on
windowsills, by doors, on steps.

759
00:49:23,379 --> 00:49:26,803
Fireworks until dawn. It celebrates
light defeating darkness.

760
00:49:26,843 --> 00:49:30,380
True, after a night of fireworks
Delhi's air gets even heavier.

761
00:49:30,420 --> 00:49:32,595
But that night, nobody thinks about it.

762
00:49:36,420 --> 00:49:37,635
And then there's cricket.

763
00:49:37,675 --> 00:49:41,501
Faiths differ here, languages differ,
but cricket belongs to almost everyone.

764
00:49:41,541 --> 00:49:44,313
A vacant lot, two sticks for
a wicket, a ball wrapped in

765
00:49:44,353 --> 00:49:46,723
electrical tape, and
twenty boys play till dark.

766
00:49:46,763 --> 00:49:50,639
And when the national team plays, the
country freezes in front of the screens.

767
00:49:50,679 --> 00:49:52,898
One last stop left. And
one answer I owe you.

768
00:50:02,890 --> 00:50:07,120
So, the riddle. How can the world's most
populous country have 'too few' babies?

769
00:50:07,160 --> 00:50:09,682
The average Indian woman
now has about one point

770
00:50:09,722 --> 00:50:11,817
nine children, by government statistics.

771
00:50:11,857 --> 00:50:13,418
Other surveys say exactly two.

772
00:50:13,458 --> 00:50:17,527
And just to keep the population from
shrinking, you need about two point one.

773
00:50:22,080 --> 00:50:23,472
Yet the population still grows.

774
00:50:23,512 --> 00:50:26,012
Because the country is very
young: a huge generation is

775
00:50:26,052 --> 00:50:27,999
just reaching the age
of starting families.

776
00:50:28,039 --> 00:50:30,493
Inertia, like a train that's
braked but still rolling.

777
00:50:30,533 --> 00:50:32,571
The UN projects growth
will stop in the early

778
00:50:32,611 --> 00:50:34,835
twenty-sixties, at about
one point seven billion.

779
00:50:34,875 --> 00:50:36,174
Then it will start to shrink.

780
00:50:36,214 --> 00:50:39,916
So the country everyone calls overpopulated
has, in fact, already hit the brakes.

781
00:50:49,110 --> 00:50:51,074
That's the India I wanted to show you.

782
00:50:51,114 --> 00:50:54,871
A country that lands on the Moon, yet
hasn't taught every child to read.

783
00:50:54,911 --> 00:50:58,563
Where tea is paid by phone, and
people pray in a river full of sewage.

784
00:50:58,603 --> 00:51:01,727
Where tigers multiply, and the
capital's air endangers life.

785
00:51:01,767 --> 00:51:05,419
India doesn't choose between poverty
and space. It lives both at once.

786
00:51:12,770 --> 00:51:14,955
See only the slums, and you miss India.

787
00:51:14,995 --> 00:51:18,378
Only the Taj Mahal, and you
miss it too. It's both. At once.

788
00:51:18,418 --> 00:51:22,144
Nearly one and a half billion
people decide daily what it becomes.

789
00:51:22,184 --> 00:51:23,456
Look at them once more.

790
00:51:28,350 --> 00:51:31,164
Morning. On the river steps,
an old man feeds birds.

791
00:51:31,204 --> 00:51:33,634
In Mumbai, the first
train is already packed.

792
00:51:33,674 --> 00:51:36,543
The tea seller puts the
kettle on, hangs up his code.

793
00:51:36,583 --> 00:51:38,848
Schoolgirls in uniform walk along a field.

794
00:51:38,888 --> 00:51:41,592
Down south, a launch site
readies the next rocket.

795
00:51:41,632 --> 00:51:44,062
All on the same morning,
in the same country.

796
00:51:50,820 --> 00:51:52,923
You know what I realised making this film?

797
00:51:52,963 --> 00:51:56,292
Almost every number about India
sounds extreme. The most populous.

798
00:51:56,332 --> 00:51:59,303
The dirtiest capital. The
biggest human gathering on Earth.

799
00:51:59,343 --> 00:52:01,293
The Moon cheaper than a Hollywood film.

800
00:52:01,333 --> 00:52:03,028
Add them up, and it's not extreme.

801
00:52:03,068 --> 00:52:06,243
It's ordinary, huge life, multiplied
by one and a half billion.

802
00:52:12,220 --> 00:52:15,669
High in the Himalayas, the ice
over Roopkund is melting again.

803
00:52:15,709 --> 00:52:18,482
The people from the
Mediterranean still lie there.

804
00:52:18,522 --> 00:52:20,733
Maybe one day we'll learn why they went.

805
00:52:23,530 --> 00:52:26,333
Your turn. Comment below:
which India would you visit?

806
00:52:26,373 --> 00:52:30,545
The Moon-landing one, or the one greeting
dawn by the river? I'm really curious.

807
00:52:34,560 --> 00:52:37,263
The next flight is already
on the board. Norway.

808
00:52:37,303 --> 00:52:40,348
Fjords, northern lights, and
a summer sun that doesn't

809
00:52:40,388 --> 00:52:42,577
set for weeks beyond the Arctic Circle.

810
00:52:42,617 --> 00:52:44,234
Thank you for flying with us.
